  "name": "passive flexion stiffness at % max flexion",
  "definition": "derivative of an exponential fit (McGill 1994 model) to moment about L4/L5 (cradle force x perpendicular distance to L4/L5) vs lumbar flexion angle (Isotrak source over sacrum, sensor over L1) normalised to Trial-1a max flexion; plus moment and angle at end of flexion",
  "unit": "mixed (see data[].unit)"
 },
 "source": {
  "short": "Parkinson 2004",
  "authors": "Parkinson RJ, Beach TAC, Callaghan JP",
  "year": 2004,
  "journal": "Clin Biomech 19:330-336",
  "doi": "10.1016/j.clinbiomech.2004.01.002",
  "n": 8,
  "sample": "8 healthy young males (university students), no activity-limiting LBP in prior year; height 1.83 (SD printed 0.38) m, mass 84.6 (10.7) kg, age 24.6 (2.1) y",
  "age": "24.6 (2.1) y",
  "sex": "8 M",
  "height_cm": 183,
  "weight_kg": 84.6
 },
 "protocol": {
  "summary": "Frictionless table: right side-lying, lower body fixed, upper body strapped to a cradle on ball bearings, arms around a column; experimenter pulled the cradle (load cell perpendicular to cradle top) into flexion over 7.5 s until end range; 3 trials per session; trials with muscle activity above threshold rejected (EMG normalised to MVC, prone rest baseline). Between sessions: lifting 4.5 kg over 55 cm barriers, 7 lifts/min, 30 min blocks (>=81% max flexion during lifting).",
  "how_to_simulate": [
   "Model side-lying with pelvis/legs fixed and upper body on a frictionless support (no gravity moment in the sagittal plane)",
   "Set all muscle activations to zero",
   "Flex the lumbar spine (L1 vs sacrum) to the model's max flexion; compute the passive moment about L4/L5",
   "Normalise angle to the max, take the slope (Nm per %) at 10, 30, 60 and 80% and the end-range moment; compare with session 1 (baseline)"
  ],
  "subject_model": {
   "sex": "M",
   "age": 24.6,
   "height_cm": 183,
   "weight_kg": 84.6,
   "disc_area_mm2": null,
   "source": "Methods 2.2 means"
  },
  "notes": "Height SD printed as 0.38 m (probably 0.038). Highest-common-moment angle (Fig 5) and highest-common-angle moment (Fig 6) not digitised. Same laboratory and protocol as Beach 2005 (different subjects)."
 },
